sections.body:
When you press the button inside a form (or press Enter in a box), the form is **submitted** and a `submit` event happens. Normally the browser reloads the page at that moment, and everything you typed is lost. `e.preventDefault()` stops this default behavior:

```js
const form = document.querySelector("#pilot-form");
form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
  e.preventDefault(); // don't reload the page
  console.log("Form submitted!");
});
```

Attach the event to the **form** (`submit`), not to the button (`click`). That way the Enter key works too.

Checking the values before accepting a form is called **validation**. Show the error message on the page so the user can see what to fix:

```js
if (name === "") {
  info.textContent = "Type the pilot's name first!";
  info.classList.add("error");
  return; // don't run the rest
}
info.classList.remove("error");
form.reset(); // empties all the boxes
```

`return` leaves the event function immediately; if there's an error, the rest doesn't run.

Tip: put the validation in a separate function like `validate(...)`. It returns the message if there's an error, or empty text `""` if not. That way you can try it with different values one by one.

examples.code:
const form = document.querySelector("#f");
const info = document.querySelector("#info");

form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  const name = document.querySelector("#name").value.trim();
  if (name === "") {
    info.textContent = "You forgot to type your name!";
    return;
  }
  info.textContent = `Welcome, Captain ${name}!`;
  form.reset();
});

const form = document.querySelector("#f");
form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
  e.preventDefault();
  const shield = document.querySelector("#shield").checked;
  const speed = Number(document.querySelector("#speed").value);
  console.log("Shield:", shield, "Speed:", speed);
  document.querySelector("#info").textContent = `Shield ${shield ? "on" : "off"}, speed ${speed * 100} km/s`;
});

